1. Purpose of the Study 
We are interested in how people experience emotions when they are presented with a 
combination of sounds and touch stimuli. You will be presented with a number of stimuli, 
and can inform us of how you feel about them. 
 
2. What Will Happen in the Experiment? 
•  You are seated in a quiet room. 
•  You will listen to a number of sound clips. 
•  At the same time, you will experience tactile stimulation in the form of textures. 
•  After each trial, you will be asked to rate how pleasant or unpleasant the 
combination felt, and how calming or exciting it was. (emotional arousal) 
•  Throughout the experiment, we will also record heart activity  and skin conductance 
using small, non-invasive sensors attached to your skin. 
The whole session will take approximately 45 minutes. 
 
3. Possible Risks and Discomforts 
•  Some sounds or tactile stimuli may feel slightly unpleasant. 
•  You are free to stop the experiment at any time without giving a reason. 
•  All stimuli are non-invasive and safe. 
 
4. Confidentiality 
•  All data will be stored securely and anonymously. 
•  No data will contain personally identifiable information. 
•  Video recordings will be analysed and deleted after the analysis is done. 
•  Your name will never appear in any report or publication. 
•  Data will only be used for scientific research purposes. 
•  Data may be kept and reused in future data 
•  Any data collected from you will be deleted if you wish. 
  
 
6. Voluntary Participation and Right to Withdraw 
•  Participation is entirely voluntary. 
•  You may withdraw from the study at any time without penalty without justification 
 
7. Incidental Findings 
During this study, your heart activity and skin conductance will be recorded for research 
purposes only. These recordings are not medical examinations and will not be routinely 
checked for health-related issues. However, in the unlikely event that a researcher notices an 
unusual or irregular pattern (for example, an irregular heartbeat), you will be informed. You 
will then be advised to consult your general practitioner or a medical professional for further 
assessment. Please note that the research team cannot provide medical diagnosis or treatment.
 
